TP.register({id:"lp9-g5",lp:9,grade:"5",mode:"baseline",nextGrade:"6th grade",build(E){const F=E.F;const FLU_TITLE="The Water Run";const FLUENCY=["The pump at Pine Lake camp sits a hundred yards up the hill from the dining hall, inside a shed with a tin roof that ticks all afternoon in the sun.","Every morning two campers walk up with the yoke and the pair of steel cans. Oriana had done it eleven times that summer, and she had worked out that the trip went easier if you filled the cans only three quarters full and made the walk twice.","The hill was the part nobody warned you about. It was not steep, exactly. It just kept going, in a long patient slope through the pines, and the cans grew heavier the whole way down without ever seeming to start.","Halfway along there was a flat rock that everybody used, whether they needed it or not. Oriana set the yoke down on it, counted to twenty, and picked it up again. Below her the roof of the dining hall showed through the branches, and somebody was already ringing the bell.","She had never once been the first pair back to the kitchen door, and somewhere around the second week of camp she had quietly stopped minding about that."].join("\n\n");const LIST_TITLE="The Logbook";const LIST=["Esperanza had kept the boathouse logbook for three summers, and she had never once missed a line. Every canoe that went out got a time, a name, and a number, and every canoe that came back got a second time written beside the first.","Her brother Ignatius thought the book was a waste of a morning. “Nobody reads it,” he said, in the middle of July.","“I read it,” said Esperanza.","In August a family at the far end of the lake asked whether anyone had seen a green paddle. Esperanza took the book down off its nail, turned back eleven pages, and found the line: canoe four, green paddle swapped for a spare, third of August. The paddle was in the rack where she had put it.","Ignatius did not say anything about that. But on the last morning of camp he took the book down himself, wrote the time, and handed it back to her without being asked."].join("\n\n");const FIRE_TITLE="Last Night at the Fire Ring";const FIRE=["By nine o'clock the lake had gone the color of old pewter, and the far shore was only a black comb of pines against a sky that had not quite given up.","Bastien sat on the end of the dock with his heels hanging over the edge. Behind him the fire ring was a small orange room with twenty people inside it, and every so often a laugh came down the hill and landed in the water beside him.","The lake at night is a drum skin. A fish turned somewhere out past the reeds and he heard it as clearly as if it had happened in his lap.","Ines came down the dock with two mugs and did not ask whether he wanted company. “Four weeks,” she said, which was how long until school, and which was also a whole answer.","The cold came up through the boards a little at a time, the way water finds a crack, and neither of them moved."].join("\n\n");const S1={title:"The Lake in Layers",src:"from <i>What a Lake Does All Year</i>, a book by Winifred Achebe",body:"By the middle of a calm summer, most deep lakes are not one body of water but three. Sun warms the top few meters, and warm water is lighter than cold water, so it floats. Below it sits a thin band where the temperature drops fast, sometimes several degrees in a single meter. Under that lies the cold layer, which stays near four degrees Celsius all summer no matter how hot the day is.\n\nWind can stir the top layer, but it cannot mix across the thin band in the middle. For three or four months the bottom of the lake and the top of the lake barely trade any water at all."};const S2={title:"Where the Oxygen Goes",src:"“Where the Oxygen Goes,” a magazine article by Noor Abadi",body:"Fish breathe oxygen that is dissolved in the water. In spring, when a lake has just mixed from top to bottom, there is oxygen through the whole of it.\n\nOnce the layers set up for the summer, the bottom layer is cut off from the air. Anything that dies in the lake drifts down into that cold water, and the bacteria that break it down use oxygen as they work. Nothing replaces it. By August the deepest water in a busy lake can hold almost none, and the fish that prefer cold water end up in the narrow band above it, where the water is still cool but the oxygen has not run out."};const S3={title:"Taking the Temperature Profile",src:"“Taking the Temperature Profile,” a science website page by Tobias Ferreira",body:"A temperature profile is a list of readings taken straight down through the water, usually one every meter. A reader lowers a sensor on a marked cable, stops at each meter, waits for the number to settle, and writes it down.\n\nPlotted on paper, the readings make a line that drops steeply in the middle. The steep part marks the thin band, and its depth tells you how thick the warm layer has grown. Repeating the profile every two weeks shows the layers forming in June and breaking down again in the fall, when the surface cools, the water weights even out, and the first strong wind mixes the whole lake from top to bottom."};const LOON_READ=["How a Loon Lives on the Water.","A loon is built for swimming and for almost nothing else. Its bones are heavier than a gull's, and its legs sit so far back on its body that it can barely walk. A loon comes ashore to nest and for very little else.","Those same legs make it a remarkable swimmer. A loon can stay under for a minute at a time and come up a long way from where it went down, which is why watching one from a dock is mostly guessing.","A loon also needs a long clear stretch of water to take off. It runs across the surface, slapping with its feet, for as much as a quarter of a mile before it lifts into the air. That is why loons nest on big lakes and not on small ponds.","Loons call at night, and the sound carries for miles across flat water. Campers who hear it once usually remember it for the rest of their lives."].join("\n\n");const WORDS=["reflection","immeasurable","transportation","disappearance","observation","inaccessible","unpredictable","submerged","reconstruct","geological","plandorous","mistravable"];const LAYER_BOX=["Sun warms the top few meters of a lake, and warm water floats on cold water.","A thin band in the middle is where the temperature drops fast, sometimes several degrees in one meter.","The cold bottom layer stays near four degrees Celsius all summer.","Wind stirs the top layer but cannot mix across the thin band.","Fish breathe oxygen that is dissolved in the water.","Once the layers set up, the bottom layer is cut off from the air.","Bacteria breaking down what sinks use up the oxygen at the bottom, and nothing replaces it.","In the fall the surface cools, the water weights even out, and the first strong wind mixes the whole lake."];const WRITE_DIR="Write on your paper. When you finish, hold it up or read it to your teacher.";const checklist=items=>`<ul class="qlist" style="margin-top:14px">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;const pills=items=>`<div class="row center" style="margin-top:18px;gap:14px;flex-wrap:wrap">${items.map(x=>`<span class="pill" style="font-size:1.2rem">${x}</span>`).join("")}</div>`;const group=(inner,label)=>`<div style="text-align:center">${inner}<p class="fig-label" style="margin:4px 0 0">${label}</p></div>`;const lookBack=(label,inner)=>`<details class="look-back" open><summary>Look back at ${label}</summary>${inner}</details>`;const factBox=(title,items)=>`<div style="border:3px dashed var(--line);border-radius:16px;padding:8px 18px;margin:14px auto 0;max-width:860px;background:var(--surface)"><p class="fig-label" style="margin:0 0 4px">${title}</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:22px;font-size:1.2rem;line-height:1.5">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul></div>`;const sourceBlock=(...list)=>`<div class="texts">${list.map((t,i)=>`<article><h3>Source ${i+1}: ${t.title}</h3><p class="small" style="margin:0 0 6px">${t.src}</p>${t.body.split(/\n\s*\n/).map(p=>`<p>${p}</p>`).join("")}</article>`).join("")}</div>`;const textBlock=(...list)=>`<div class="texts">${list.map((t,i)=>`<article><h3>Text ${i+1}: ${t.title}</h3>${t.body.split(/\n\s*\n/).map(p=>`<p>${p}</p>`).join("")}</article>`).join("")}</div>`;function areaModel(){const ox=104,oy=42,S=250,cw=S/5,rh=S/3,W=ox+S+76,H=oy+S+50;let s=`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${W} ${H}" width="${W}" style="max-width:100%;height:auto" role="img" aria-label="A one meter by one meter square cut into 5 columns and 3 rows, making 15 equal parts. The block that is 3 columns wide and 2 rows tall is shaded.">`;for(let r=0;r<3;r++)for(let c=0;c<5;c++)s+=`<rect x="${ox+c*cw}" y="${oy+r*rh}" width="${cw}" height="${rh}" fill="${r<2&&c<3?"var(--sea-soft)":"var(--surface)"}" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2"/>`;const t=(x,y,txt,an)=>`<text x="${x}" y="${y}" text-anchor="${an||"middle"}" font-size="19" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)">${txt}</text>`;s+=`<line x1="${ox}" y1="${oy-12}" x2="${ox+3*cw}" y2="${oy-12}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/>`+t(ox+1.5*cw,oy-20,"3/5 m");s+=`<line x1="${ox-14}" y1="${oy}" x2="${ox-14}" y2="${oy+2*rh}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/>`+t(ox-22,oy+rh+6,"2/3 m","end");s+=t(ox+S/2,oy+S+32,"1 m")+t(ox+S+12,oy+S/2+6,"1 m","start");return s+"</svg>"}const STOPS=[{id:1,title:"Crate Label Words",short:"Label Words",parts:[{std:["RF.5.3"],kind:"words",words:WORDS,n:12,dur:65,dir:"Read each word out loud. The last two are made-up words. Just sound them out.",desc:"reading long words using prefixes, suffixes, roots, and syllable chunks",key:["Read each word in order. plandorous and mistravable are made-up words, so the student has to use chunks and word parts instead of memory: plan-dor-ous, mis-trav-a-ble.","Watch for the prefixes re-, im-, trans-, dis-, in-, un-, sub-, and mis-, the suffixes -tion, -able, -ance, -ible, -ed, -ous, and -ical, and the roots flect for bend, measur, port for carry, serv for watch, cess for go, dict for say, merg for dip, struct for build, and geo for earth.","Watch whether the student breaks a long word into syllable chunks (trans-por-ta-tion, in-ac-ces-si-ble, un-pre-dict-a-ble, ge-o-log-i-cal) or guesses from the first few letters."]}]},{id:2,title:"Read the Water Run",short:"Read Aloud",parts:[{std:["RF.5.4"],kind:"read",n:0,label:"words read correctly",dur:95,dir:"Read the story out loud at your normal speed. Use your best reading voice.",desc:"a grade-level passage read aloud",html:()=>F.passage(FLUENCY,FLU_TITLE),key:["__WC__ words, not counting the title. Time 1 minute, mark the last word read, and count errors on your own copy. Let the student finish the passage.","Spring grade 5 benchmark: about 146 words correct per minute, from the Hasbrouck and Tindal 2017 spring 50th percentile. For the 25th and 10th percentile rows, read them off your own copy of that table rather than from here.","Note pace, phrasing, and expression. Listen for the pauses at the commas in the long first sentence, and for the three short sentences in the third paragraph, which should not run together."]}]},{id:3,title:"Story Questions",short:"Story Qs",parts:[{title:"Quote It",std:["RL.5.1"],dur:105,dir:"Read the story. Then answer out loud. Read me the exact words from the story that prove each answer.",desc:"quoting a story accurately to tell what it says and to back up inferences",html:()=>F.passage(LIST,LIST_TITLE,true),qs:["How long had Esperanza kept the logbook, and how well? Read me the exact words.","What goes in the book for each canoe? Read me the words that tell you.","What did she find in the book in August, and how far back was it? Read me the exact words.","The story never says that Ignatius changed his mind about the book. Read me words that show it anyway.","Did Ignatius ever say out loud that he was wrong? Read me the exact words that prove your answer."],key:["Three summers, without missing a line. Quotes: “Esperanza had kept the boathouse logbook for three summers, and she had never once missed a line.” (RL.5.1, explicit)","Quotes: “Every canoe that went out got a time, a name, and a number, and every canoe that came back got a second time written beside the first.” (RL.5.1, explicit)","Eleven pages back. Quotes: “turned back eleven pages, and found the line: canoe four, green paddle swapped for a spare, third of August.” (RL.5.1, explicit)","Quotes: “on the last morning of camp he took the book down himself, wrote the time, and handed it back to her without being asked.” Doing the job himself is the evidence. (RL.5.1, inference backed by a quote)","No. Quotes: “Ignatius did not say anything about that.” He changed what he did, not what he said. (RL.5.1, inference backed by a quote)"]},{title:"Word Pictures",std:["RL.5.4"],dur:90,dir:"Read the second story. Then answer out loud.",desc:"explaining similes, metaphors, and words as they are used in a story",html:()=>F.passage(FIRE,FIRE_TITLE,true),qs:["“the lake had gone the color of old pewter.” What does that tell you about the water?","“the far shore was only a black comb of pines.” Simile or metaphor? What does it show you?","“the fire ring was a small orange room with twenty people inside it.” Is there a real room? What does the author mean?","“The lake at night is a drum skin.” What does that tell you about the sound?","“a laugh came down the hill and landed in the water beside him.” What really happened?","What does turned mean in “A fish turned somewhere out past the reeds”?"],key:["Pewter is a dull gray metal, so the water had gone flat and metallic gray as the light drained out of the sky. (RL.5.4, meaning of a phrase as used in the text)","A metaphor, because it says the shore was a comb, with no like or as. The pine tops stood in an even row of dark points against the sky, the way the teeth of a comb do. (RL.5.4, metaphor)","No real room. The firelight lit a closed circle that felt warm and walled in, with everyone gathered inside it, so the author calls it a room. (RL.5.4, metaphor)","A metaphor. Flat water carries sound tightly and loudly, the way a stretched drumhead does, so a small splash far away sounded as though it happened right next to him. (RL.5.4, metaphor)","The sound of laughing carried down from the fire ring across the water, and he heard it so clearly that it seemed to arrive beside him. Nothing really fell in. (RL.5.4, figurative language)","Rolled or flipped over at the surface. A fish breaking the surface is what made the sound he heard. (RL.5.4, meaning of a word as used in the text)"]}]},{id:4,title:"Three Lake Texts",short:"Three Texts",parts:[{title:"Quote the Text",std:["RI.5.1"],dur:95,dir:"Read all three texts to yourself. Then answer out loud with the exact words from the text.",desc:"quoting an informational text accurately to tell what it says and to back up inferences",html:()=>textBlock(S1,S2,S3),qs:["How many layers does a calm summer lake have? Read me the exact words.","Why does the warm water sit on top? Read me the words that tell you.","How cold does the bottom layer stay in summer? Read me the exact words.","Does wind mix the whole lake in July? Read me the words that back up your answer.","How does a reader take a temperature profile? Read me the words that describe it."],key:["Three. Quotes: “most deep lakes are not one body of water but three.” (RI.5.1, explicit)","Quotes: “warm water is lighter than cold water, so it floats.” (RI.5.1, explicit)","Quotes: “stays near four degrees Celsius all summer no matter how hot the day is.” (RI.5.1, explicit)","No. Quotes: “Wind can stir the top layer, but it cannot mix across the thin band in the middle.” (RI.5.1, inference backed by a quote)","Quotes: “lowers a sensor on a marked cable, stops at each meter, waits for the number to settle, and writes it down.” (RI.5.1, explicit)"]},{title:"Put It Together",std:["RI.5.9"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Answer out loud. Use facts from more than one text in each answer.",desc:"putting together information from several texts on the same topic",html:()=>lookBack("the three texts",textBlock(S1,S2,S3)),qs:["Use all three texts. Explain the chain from a sunny day in June to a cold-water fish crowded into a narrow band in August.","A reader's profile in October shows nearly the same temperature at every meter. Use at least two texts to explain what has happened, and what it means for the oxygen at the bottom.","Which text would you use to find out how the layers are measured. Which two to explain why the bottom runs out of oxygen? Tell why."],key:["Text 1: the sun warms the top few meters, warm water floats, and a thin band forms where the temperature drops fast, which wind cannot mix across. Text 2: once the layers set up, the bottom layer is cut off from the air, and the bacteria breaking down what sinks use the oxygen with nothing to replace it, so by August the deepest water holds almost none and cold-water fish end up in the narrow band above it. Text 3: the profile every two weeks is what shows the layers forming in June. Credit an answer that draws on all three. (RI.5.9)","Text 3: in the fall the surface cools, the water weights even out, and the first strong wind mixes the whole lake top to bottom, so the steep part of the line is gone. Text 1: those layers were the only thing keeping the top and bottom from trading water. Text 2: the bottom is cut off from the air only while the layers hold, so once the lake mixes there is oxygen through the whole of it again, the way there is in spring. (RI.5.9)","Text 3 for how the layers are measured, because it is the only one that describes the sensor on a marked cable and the steep part of the plotted line. Texts 1 and 2 for why the bottom runs out: Text 1 explains the thin band that wind cannot cross, and Text 2 explains that the bacteria use the oxygen and nothing replaces it. (RI.5.9)"]}]},{id:5,title:"Oxygen Notes",short:"Notes",parts:[{std:["W.5.8"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:110,dir:"Read both sources. Write your notes on your paper and hold them up.",desc:"notes in the student's own words from two sources, a list of sources, and a spoken summary",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="font-size:1.5rem;margin-top:0">Question: why does the deepest water in a lake run short of oxygen by the end of the summer?</p>${sourceBlock(S2,S3)}<ol class="qlist"><li>Write 3 short notes that answer the question. Use your own words. A note is a few words, not a whole sentence. Use both sources.</li><li>Under your notes, list your sources. Write the title and the author of each one.</li><li>Now look only at your notes. Answer the question out loud in one or two sentences.</li></ol>`,key:["Writes 3 notes that answer the question and draw on both sources, such as: the bottom layer is cut off from the air; dead material sinks into the cold water; bacteria use oxygen breaking it down; nothing replaces it; cold-water fish move up into a narrow band; a profile every two weeks tracks the layers; the lake mixes again in the fall. (W.5.8, gathers relevant information)","Notes are short and in the student's own words, not sentences copied out of the sources. (W.5.8, paraphrases in notes)","Lists both sources by title and author: “Where the Oxygen Goes” by Noor Abadi, and “Taking the Temperature Profile” by Tobias Ferreira. (W.5.8, provides a list of sources)","Sums the answer up out loud from the notes, in the student's own words, with the facts correct: once the layers set up, the bottom is sealed off from the air, and the bacteria working on what sinks use the oxygen up with nothing to replace it. (W.5.8, summarizes in finished work)"]}]},{id:6,title:"Listen at the Fire Ring",short:"Listen",parts:[{std:["SL.5.2"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:90,dir:"Listen while your teacher reads. Then sum it up out loud in your own words.",desc:"summarizing a text read aloud",read:LOON_READ,html:()=>`<p class="huge" aria-hidden="true">${F.icon("duck",96)}</p><p class="big" style="text-align:center">Listen carefully. Your teacher will read you a short report about loons, the birds that call across the lake at night. Then sum it up in two or three sentences.</p>`,qs:["Sum the whole report up in two or three sentences.","Put the part about the loon's legs in your own words. Why do they matter twice over?","Sum up just the part about taking off.","Name one detail you left out of your summary, and tell me why you left it out."],key:["Gives the main idea in the student's own words: a loon's body is built for swimming rather than for land or for easy flight, which shapes where it can live. (SL.5.2, summarizes a text read aloud)","Paraphrases both halves: the legs sit so far back that the bird can barely walk, and those same legs make it a powerful swimmer that can stay under about a minute and surface far away. (SL.5.2, key details in the student's own words)","Paraphrases: a loon has to run across the water slapping its feet, for up to a quarter of a mile, before it can lift off, which is why loons nest on big lakes rather than small ponds. (SL.5.2)","Names something genuinely minor and explains the choice, such as the heavier bones compared with a gull's, or the night calling carrying for miles. A summary should keep the main ideas and drop the small detail. (SL.5.2, tells main ideas from details)"]}]},{id:7,title:"Opinion Talk",short:"Opinion",parts:[{std:["SL.5.4"],n:6,label:"checks met",dur:85,dir:"Pick one question. Plan for one minute. Then give your opinion and your reasons out loud.",desc:"presenting an opinion out loud with reasons, details, and a clear closing",html:()=>`<p class="big">Pick one and tell me what you think. Take one minute to plan.</p><ul class="qlist" style="margin-top:14px"><li>Should a camp keep a paper logbook now that a phone could do the same job?</li><li>Is a rainy day at camp a wasted day?</li></ul>${pills(["My opinion","Reason 1","Reason 2","For example","So..."])}`,key:["States a clear opinion (SL.5.4)","Gives reasons in a logical order (SL.5.4)","Backs at least one reason with a fact or an example (SL.5.4)","Uses linking words such as for example, also, because of this, therefore (SL.5.4)","Ends with a concluding statement rather than trailing off (SL.5.4)","Speaks clearly at a pace you can follow (SL.5.4)"]}]},{id:8,title:"Logbook Fix-Ups",short:"Logbook Fix",parts:[{std:["L.5.1"],n:6,dur:80,dir:"Each logbook line needs one fix. Say the whole sentence out loud the right way.",desc:"spoken grammar items (verb tense shifts, perfect tenses, paired conjunctions, parts of speech)",qs:["Last night the crew carried the canoes up, stacks them on the rack, and ties the tarp down.","Since June, the boathouse log has ran out of pages twice.","By the time the rain started, the crew already pulled the last canoe in.","Not only was the spare paddle missing, and the bailer was gone too.","Say this with the right form of the verb log: “By Friday, the counselors ____ every canoe trip of the summer.”","“Whew, the canoe slid off the rack and onto the grass!” Find the interjection, a preposition, and the conjunction."],key:["Last night the crew carried the canoes up, stacked them on the rack, and tied the tarp down. (L.5.1, correcting a shift in verb tense)","Since June, the boathouse log has run out of pages twice. (L.5.1, present perfect tense)","By the time the rain started, the crew had already pulled the last canoe in. (L.5.1, past perfect tense)","Not only was the spare paddle missing, but the bailer was also gone. (L.5.1, correlative conjunctions not only and but also)","By Friday, the counselors will have logged every canoe trip of the summer. (L.5.1, future perfect tense)","Interjection: Whew. Preposition: off, or onto. Conjunction: and. (L.5.1, parts of speech and their function in a sentence)"]}]},{id:9,title:"Ten Times and One Tenth",short:"Place Value",parts:[{std:["5.NBT.A.1"],n:6,dur:60,dir:"Answer out loud. Explain how you know.",desc:"a digit worth 10 times the place to its right and 1/10 of the place to its left",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Camp counts paddle strokes, meters of rope, and liters of water, so the same digit turns up in different places all day.</p>`,fig:()=>`<p class="huge" style="margin:0">4,400</p><p class="small" style="text-align:center;margin-top:0">paddle strokes on the long crossing</p>`,qs:["In 4,400, how does the 4 in the thousands place compare to the 4 in the hundreds place?","In 2,200, the 2 in the hundreds place is worth what part of the 2 in the thousands place?","In 0.77, how does the 7 in the tenths place compare to the 7 in the hundredths place?","A 9 in the ones place is worth 9. In which place would a 9 be worth one tenth of that, and what is it worth there?","Use place value to explain why 3,000 ÷ 300 = 10.","In 66.6, compare each 6 to the 6 just to its right."],key:["It is worth 10 times as much. The 4 in the thousands place is 4,000 and the 4 in the hundreds place is 400, and 4,000 is 10 times 400. Partial: naming the two values with no comparison is approaching. (5.NBT.A.1)","One tenth of it. 200 is 1/10 of 2,000. Partial: 10 times as much reverses the comparison and does not count. (5.NBT.A.1)","It is worth 10 times as much. The 7 in the tenths place is 0.7 and the 7 in the hundredths place is 0.07, and 0.7 is 10 times 0.07. Partial: naming the two values with no comparison is approaching. (5.NBT.A.1)","The tenths place, where a 9 is worth 0.9. 0.9 is one tenth of 9. Partial: naming the place with no value given is approaching. (5.NBT.A.1)","The 3 in the thousands place is worth 10 times the 3 in the hundreds place, so 3,000 is 10 groups of 300. That is why the quotient is 10. Partial: working the division out with no place value reason is approaching. (5.NBT.A.1)","The 6 in the tens place is 60 and the 6 in the ones place is 6, so 60 is 10 times 6. The 6 in the ones place is 6 and the 6 in the tenths place is 0.6, so 6 is 10 times 0.6. Each 6 is worth 10 times the 6 one place to its right. Partial: one of the two comparisons given is approaching. (5.NBT.A.1)"]}]},{id:10,title:"Thousandths of a Meter",short:"Decimals",parts:[{std:["5.NBT.A.3"],n:6,dur:80,dir:"Say numbers 1 and 2 out loud. Write numbers 3 through 6 on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"reading, writing, and comparing decimals to thousandths",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">The depth sensor is lowered on a cable measured to the nearest thousandth of a meter. So every number in the log has three places after the decimal point.</p>`,qs:["Read this number out loud: 3.407","Say 0.085 in words.","Write this as a decimal number: five and twenty-three thousandths","Write 6.314 in expanded form.","Write &gt;, &lt;, or = to make it true: 0.5 ____ 0.47","Put these in order from least to greatest: 0.8, 0.08, 0.88, 0.808"],key:["Three and four hundred seven thousandths. “Three point four zero seven” on its own does not meet the standard, which asks for the number name. Partial: three point four zero seven is approaching, since the place value name is what is asked. (5.NBT.A.3)","Eighty-five thousandths. Partial: zero point zero eight five is approaching, since the place value name is what is asked. (5.NBT.A.3)","5.023. Twenty-three thousandths fills the hundredths and thousandths places, so a zero holds the tenths place. Partial: 5.23 leaves no zero holding the tenths place and does not count. (5.NBT.A.3)","6 + 0.3 + 0.01 + 0.004. Accept (6 × 1) + (3 × 1/10) + (1 × 1/100) + (4 × 1/1000). Partial: three of the four places written is approaching. (5.NBT.A.3, expanded form)","0.5 &gt; 0.47. Line the places up: 0.50 against 0.47, and 5 tenths beats 4 tenths. Or: compare 50 hundredths against 47 hundredths. Partial: the right choice with the sign the wrong way round is approaching. (5.NBT.A.3)","0.08, 0.8, 0.808, 0.88. Written to three places they are 0.080, 0.800, 0.808, and 0.880. Partial: three of the four in order is approaching. (5.NBT.A.3)"]}]},{id:11,title:"Inside the Brackets",short:"Brackets",parts:[{std:["5.OA.A.1"],n:6,dur:85,dir:"Solve each one on your paper. Show the step inside the brackets first, then hold your paper up.",desc:"using parentheses and brackets in expressions and finding their value",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">The camp cooks work out their orders on paper before they phone them in. Find the value of each expression.</p>`,qs:["7 × (8 + 5) = ?","(36 ÷ 4) + (6 × 3) = ?","3 × [20 − (4 × 3)] = ?","50 − [(7 + 2) × 5] = ?","Write an expression with parentheses for this: add 9 and 6, then multiply the sum by 4. Then find its value.","Does 5 × (12 − 3) equal 5 × 12 − 3? Find both values and say which one is greater."],key:["91. Inside first: 8 + 5 = 13, then 7 × 13 = 91. Partial: 61 comes from multiplying before adding and does not count. (5.OA.A.1)","27. 36 ÷ 4 = 9 and 6 × 3 = 18, then 9 + 18 = 27. Partial: one of the two parentheses worked out is approaching. (5.OA.A.1)","24. Innermost first: 4 × 3 = 12. Then 20 − 12 = 8, and 3 × 8 = 24. Partial: 8 found by stopping inside the brackets is approaching. (5.OA.A.1)","5. Innermost first: 7 + 2 = 9. Then 9 × 5 = 45, and 50 − 45 = 5. Partial: 45 found by stopping inside the brackets is approaching. (5.OA.A.1)","4 × (9 + 6), which equals 4 × 15 = 60. Accept (9 + 6) × 4. Partial: 60 given with no expression written is approaching. (5.OA.A.1, writing an expression with parentheses)","No, they are not equal. 5 × (12 − 3) = 5 × 9 = 45, and 5 × 12 − 3 = 60 − 3 = 57. The second one, 5 × 12 − 3, is greater. Partial: saying they are not equal with only one value worked out is approaching. (5.OA.A.1)"]}]},{id:12,title:"Camp Fractions",short:"Fractions",parts:[{title:"Add and Subtract",std:["5.NF.A.1"],n:5,dur:90,dir:"Solve on your paper. Show your work and hold it up.",desc:"adding and subtracting fractions and mixed numbers with unlike denominators",qs:["2/5 + 1/3 = ?","7/8 − 1/6 = ?","1 1/4 + 2 2/3 = ?","3 1/2 − 1 3/4 = ?","5/6 + 3/4 = ?"],key:["11/15. In fifteenths that is 6/15 + 5/15. Or: thirtieths work too, 12/30 + 10/30 = 22/30. Partial: 3/8 comes from adding across and does not count. (5.NF.A.1)","17/24. In twenty-fourths that is 21/24 − 4/24. Partial: subtracting across, numerator from numerator and denominator from denominator, does not count. (5.NF.A.1)","3 11/12. In twelfths that is 1 3/12 + 2 8/12. Or: change both to improper fractions, 15/12 + 32/12 = 47/12. (5.NF.A.1)","1 3/4. As fourths that is 14/4 − 7/4 = 7/4. Or: regroup instead, 3 2/4 becomes 2 6/4, then take 1 3/4 away. (5.NF.A.1)","19/12, which is 1 7/12. In twelfths that is 10/12 + 9/12. Partial: 19/12 right with no mixed number given is approaching. (5.NF.A.1)"]},{title:"Fraction Word Problems",std:["5.NF.A.2"],n:4,dur:70,dir:"Solve the first two on your paper. Explain the last two out loud.",desc:"fraction word problems and benchmark fraction checks",qs:["The trail crew cleared 5/8 of a mile before lunch and 3/4 of a mile after lunch. How far did they clear in all?","A water jug held 2 1/2 liters. The cooks used 3/4 of a liter. How much is left?","Without solving: Omar says 5/6 − 1/3 = 4/3. How can you tell he is wrong?","Without solving exactly: is 3/8 + 4/9 more or less than 1? Use benchmarks to explain."],key:["11/8 of a mile, which is 1 3/8 miles. In eighths that is 5/8 + 6/8. Partial: 11/8 right with no mixed number given is approaching. (5.NF.A.2)","1 3/4 liters. In fourths that is 10/4 − 3/4 = 7/4. Or: take 1/2 off 2 1/2 to get 2, then take the other 1/4 off to get 1 3/4. (5.NF.A.2)","4/3 is more than 1, and he started from 5/6, which is already less than 1, then took something away. The answer has to be smaller than 5/6, not bigger than a whole. Partial: saying he is wrong with no benchmark used is approaching. (5.NF.A.2, benchmark check)","Less than 1. 3/8 is less than 1/2 because half of 8 is 4, and 4/9 is less than 1/2 because half of 9 is 4 1/2. Two amounts that are each under a half cannot add to a whole. Partial: saying less with only one of the two fractions compared is approaching. (5.NF.A.2, benchmark fractions)"]}]},{id:13,title:"Share the Supplies",short:"Shares",parts:[{title:"Fractions as Division",std:["5.NF.B.3"],n:5,dur:70,dir:"Solve on your paper. Then tell your teacher your answers.",desc:"a fraction as division, with sharing problems that give fraction and mixed number answers",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Everything at camp gets split into equal shares. Solve these.</p>`,qs:["7 loaves of camp bread are shared equally among 8 campers. How much of a loaf does each camper get?","Write 23 ÷ 4 as a fraction and as a mixed number.","A 26 kilogram crate of food is split equally among 4 tents. How many kilograms does each tent get, and between which two whole numbers is your answer?","4 liters of drinking water are poured equally into 5 bottles. How much goes in each one? Tell how you know the answer is less than 1 liter.","9 meters of rope are cut into 10 equal pieces. How long is each piece? Write it as a fraction and as a decimal."],key:["7/8 of a loaf. 7 ÷ 8 = 7/8. Partial: 8/7 reverses the share and does not count. (5.NF.B.3)","23/4 and 5 3/4, because 4 × 5 = 20 with 3 left over. Partial: one of the two forms given is approaching. (5.NF.B.3)","26/4 kilograms, which is 13/2 or 6 1/2 kilograms. It lies between 6 and 7. Partial: 26/4 right with no pair of whole numbers named is approaching. (5.NF.B.3)","4/5 of a liter. There are fewer liters than bottles, so each bottle has to get less than a whole liter. Partial: 4/5 right with no reason that it is less than 1 is approaching. (5.NF.B.3)","9/10 of a meter, which is 0.9 meter. Partial: one of the fraction and the decimal given is approaching. (5.NF.B.3)"]},{title:"Multiply Fractions",std:["5.NF.B.4"],n:6,dur:75,dir:"Solve on your paper. For number 6, use the picture. Then tell your teacher your answers.",desc:"multiplying a whole number by a fraction and a fraction by a fraction, with an area model",qs:["2/3 × 9 = ?","3/4 × 2/5 = ?","5/8 × 32 = ?","Camp folded 40 fire starters. 3/5 of them went out to the far campsite. How many went out there?","5/6 × 3/4 = ?","Look at the picture. The big square is one square meter of a tent pad. The shaded patch is 3/5 of a meter along the bottom and 2/3 of a meter up the side. Write a multiplication equation for the area of the patch and give the answer."],fig2:()=>F.row([areaModel(),"One square meter of tent pad, cut into 15 equal parts"]),key:["6. Write 9 as 9/1 and multiply across: 2 × 9 = 18 over 3, and 18/3 = 6. Or: 9 ÷ 3 = 3, then 3 × 2 = 6. (5.NF.B.4)","6/20, which is the same as 3/10. Or: cancel the 2 into the 4 first, which leaves 3/10. (5.NF.B.4)","20. 32 ÷ 8 = 4, and 4 × 5 = 20. Or: 5/8 × 32 = 160/8 = 20. (5.NF.B.4)","24 fire starters. 40 ÷ 5 = 8, and 8 × 3 = 24. Or: 3/5 × 40 = 120/5 = 24. (5.NF.B.4)","15/24, which is the same as 5/8. Or: cancel the 3 into the 6 first, which leaves 5/8. (5.NF.B.4)","3/5 × 2/3 = 6/15, which is 2/5 of a square meter. The square is cut into 5 columns and 3 rows, giving 15 equal parts, and the shaded patch covers 6 of them. Partial: the equation right with no area given is approaching. (5.NF.B.4, area model for a fraction times a fraction)"]}]},{id:14,title:"Pack the Crates",short:"Volume",parts:[{title:"What Volume Is",std:["5.MD.C.3"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Look at the stack of cubes. Answer out loud.",desc:"counting unit cubes for volume and telling what volume measures",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Folded tarps are packed in wooden crates. A crate is a rectangular prism, and the packs inside sit in neat layers.</p>`,fig:()=>F.row([F.prism(5,3,2),"A crate 5 cubes long, 3 cubes deep, and 2 cubes tall"]),qs:["Each cube is 1 cubic unit. What is the volume of this stack? Say the unit.","Why do we measure volume in cubic units instead of flat square units?","A solid is packed full with 18 unit cubes and no gaps. What is its volume?"],key:["30 cubic units. Partial: 30 with no unit named is approaching. (5.MD.C.3)","Because the cubes fill the solid in three directions, long, deep, and tall, with no gaps and no overlaps, so the measuring unit has to be a cube. A flat square only covers a surface. Partial: naming the three directions with nothing said about a flat square covering only a surface is approaching. (5.MD.C.3)","18 cubic units. The number of unit cubes that pack a solid with no gaps is its volume. Partial: 18 with no unit named is approaching. (5.MD.C.3)"]},{title:"Find the Volume",std:["5.MD.C.5"],n:6,dur:80,dir:"Solve on your paper and hold it up. Say the unit with every answer.",desc:"volume of rectangular prisms, found by multiplying and by adding",qs:["A food crate is 9 inches long, 6 inches wide, and 4 inches tall. What is its volume?","Look at the crate in the picture above. It holds 2 layers of unit cubes, and each layer is 5 by 3. How many cubes fit in all? Write it two different ways.","A storage bin has a volume of 96 cubic feet. Its base is 8 feet by 3 feet. How tall is the bin?","Two crates sit side by side. One is 4 by 3 by 3 units and the other is 6 by 2 by 3 units. What is their total volume?","A water tank is a cube 12 centimeters along every edge. What is its volume?","Crate A is 2 by 8 by 3 feet. Crate B is 4 by 4 by 3 feet. Which crate holds more?"],key:["216 cubic inches. 9 × 6 × 4 = 216. Or: 9 × 6 = 54 for the base, then 54 × 4. (5.MD.C.5)","30 cubes. One layer is 5 × 3 = 15, and 15 × 2 = 30. Written the other way, 5 × 3 × 2 = 30. Partial: one of the two ways written is approaching. (5.MD.C.5, volume as repeated layers and as a product of three edges)","4 feet. The base covers 8 × 3 = 24 square feet, and 96 ÷ 24 = 4. Or: 96 ÷ 8 = 12, then 12 ÷ 3 = 4. (5.MD.C.5)","72 cubic units. 4 × 3 × 3 = 36 and 6 × 2 × 3 = 36, then 36 + 36 = 72. Volume adds when solids do not overlap. Partial: one of the two crates worked out is approaching. (5.MD.C.5, volume is additive)","1,728 cubic centimeters. 12 × 12 × 12 = 1,728. Partial: 1,728 with no unit named is approaching. (5.MD.C.5)","Neither. Both hold 48 cubic feet, because 2 × 8 × 3 = 48 and 4 × 4 × 3 = 48. Partial: naming neither with only one volume worked out is approaching. (5.MD.C.5)"]}]},{id:15,title:"The Camp Map Grid",short:"Grid",parts:[{std:["5.G.A.1"],n:6,dur:65,dir:"Look at the map grid. Answer out loud.",desc:"naming the origin, the axes, and the coordinates of points on a grid",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">This grid is the camp map. T is the tent field, D is the dock, and R is the fire ring. One unit on the grid is one hundred steps.</p>`,fig:()=>F.row([F.coordGrid({min:0,max:8,size:380,points:[[3,6,"T"],[7,2,"D"],[5,0,"R"]]}),"The camp map"]),qs:["Find the spot where the two axes cross. What is that point called, and what are its coordinates?","What are the coordinates of the tent field, point T?","Which place is at (7, 2)?","To walk from the origin to the dock, do you go across first or up first? How do you know?","The fire ring, point R, is at (5, 0). Why is the second number 0?","A new bench goes 2 units to the right of the tent field and 1 unit up from it. What are its coordinates?"],key:["The origin, at (0, 0), where the x-axis and the y-axis cross. Partial: naming the origin with no coordinates given is approaching. (5.G.A.1)","(3, 6). Partial: (6, 3) reverses the pair and is approaching. (5.G.A.1)","D, the dock. Partial: naming the place at (2, 7) instead reads the pair in the wrong order and is approaching. (5.G.A.1)","Across first, 7 units along the x-axis, because the first number in the pair is the x-coordinate. Then 2 units up the y-axis. Partial: saying across first with no reason from the first number is approaching. (5.G.A.1)","It sits right on the x-axis, so there is no move up the y-axis at all. Partial: naming the x-axis with nothing said about not moving up is approaching. (5.G.A.1)","(5, 7). Start at (3, 6), add 2 to the x-coordinate and 1 to the y-coordinate. Partial: one of the two coordinates right is approaching. (5.G.A.1)"]}]},{id:16,title:"Camp Writer",short:"Write",parts:[{alt:{group:"write",key:"opinion",label:"Opinion",icon:"💬",hint:"Dock or shoreline path?"},std:["W.5.1","L.5.1"],n:6,label:"checks met",dur:230,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"an opinion writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">Camp has money for one project this summer. It can rebuild the swimming dock, which is old and splintery, or it can rebuild the muddy shoreline path with stone steps and planting so the bank stops washing into the lake every time it rains.</p><p class="big">Which should camp pick? Write your opinion with reasons. Write 6 to 8 sentences.</p>${checklist(["Introduce the topic and state your opinion clearly","Put your reasons in a logical order","Back up each reason with a fact or a detail","Link your opinion and reasons: for example, specifically, because of this, consequently","End with a concluding statement"])}`,key:["Introduces the topic and states a clear opinion (W.5.1)","Reasons are ordered logically rather than listed at random (W.5.1)","Reasons are supported with facts or details, such as a splintery dock, or soil washing off the bank into the water (W.5.1)","Links the opinion and the reasons with words and phrases such as for example, specifically, because of this, consequently (W.5.1)","Ends with a concluding statement (W.5.1)","Verb tenses stay consistent and correct across the piece (L.5.1)"]},{alt:{group:"write",key:"facts",label:"Explain",icon:"📋",hint:"Why a lake has layers"},std:["W.5.2","L.5.1"],n:6,label:"checks met",dur:230,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"an informative writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">Write a page that teaches a reader why a deep lake splits into layers in summer, and what that does to the oxygen at the bottom. Use at least three facts from the fact box, in your own words. Write 6 to 8 sentences.</p>${factBox("Fact box: a lake in summer",LAYER_BOX)}${checklist(["Start by naming your topic","Put facts that go together next to each other","Use linking words: also, another, for example, because, as a result","Use the topic words correctly: layer, dissolved oxygen, bacteria, mix","End with a closing sentence"])}`,key:["Introduces the topic clearly, and a title or heading counts as formatting (W.5.2)","Groups related information in an order that makes sense, such as the layers first and the oxygen after (W.5.2)","Develops the topic with at least three facts from the box, written in the student's own sentences (W.5.2)","Links ideas with words and phrases such as also, another, for example, because, as a result (W.5.2)","Uses the topic words layer, dissolved oxygen, bacteria, and mix correctly (W.5.2)","Ends with a concluding sentence, and verb tenses stay consistent (W.5.2, L.5.1)"]},{alt:{group:"write",key:"story",label:"Story",icon:"✨",hint:"After the fire goes out"},std:["W.5.3","L.5.1"],n:6,label:"checks met",dur:230,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"a narrative writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">On the last night of camp, everyone walks down to the dock after the fire has burned out, to listen to the lake for half an hour before bed. You are one of them. Write the story of that half hour. It can be real or made up. Write 7 to 9 sentences.</p>${pills(["Set the scene","Then","After a while","By the end"])}${checklist(["Set the scene and tell who is there","Tell the events in a clear order, with transition words","Use dialogue, or a description of what someone thinks and feels","Use concrete words and sensory detail so the reader can picture it","Give the story an ending that follows from the events"])}`,key:["Orients the reader: who is there, where they are, and what is happening (W.5.3)","Events unfold in a clear order, helped by transition words such as then, after a while, by the end (W.5.3)","Uses dialogue or description to show what a character thinks or feels (W.5.3)","Uses concrete words and sensory detail about the lake, the dark, or the sounds (W.5.3)","Ends with a conclusion that follows from the events (W.5.3)","Verb tenses stay consistent across the piece (L.5.1)"]}]},{id:17,title:"The Ring on the Rock",short:"Tiny Bits",parts:[{std:["5-PS1-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"using a model of particles too small to be seen to explain what happens to matter",html:()=>`<p class="big">A hollow in a rock by the lake fills with lake water after a storm. A week of sun later the hollow is dry, and a pale crusty ring is left around the inside.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("drop",84)}${F.icon("sun",84)}${F.icon("wave",84)}</div>`,qs:["Where did the water go, and what is the pale ring?","The ring was invisible while the water was there. Explain that using particles too small to see.","Camp mugs smell faintly of the soup cooked an hour ago even after rinsing. Explain that the same way."],key:["The water went into the air as water vapor, which is water as particles far too small to see. The ring is the minerals that were dissolved in the lake water and stayed behind when the water left. (5-PS1-1)","The minerals were broken into particles far too small to see and spread out among the water particles, so they could not be seen. Once the water left, so many of them were crowded together on the rock that they became visible as a crust. (5-PS1-1)","Particles from the soup are held in the mug's surface and keep leaving it into the air, spreading out until some reach your nose. They are far too small to see or rinse off completely. (5-PS1-1)"]},{std:["5-PS1-3"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"identifying materials from measured properties rather than from appearance",html:()=>`<p class="big">Four gray rings turned up in the camp lost property box. All four are the same size.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Ring</th><th>Mass</th><th>Floats?</th><th>Pulled by a magnet?</th><th>Scratched by a steel key?</th></tr>
     <tr><td>A</td><td>6 g</td><td>yes</td><td>no</td><td>yes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>B</td><td>24 g</td><td>no</td><td>yes</td><td>no</td></tr>
     <tr><td>C</td><td>9 g</td><td>no</td><td>no</td><td>yes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>D</td><td>26 g</td><td>no</td><td>no</td><td>no</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The four rings are plastic, steel, aluminum, and stainless steel that is not magnetic.</p>`,qs:["Which ring is the plastic, and name two results that show it.","Rings B and D are close in mass and neither is scratched by a key. Which is the magnetic steel, and which test tells them apart?","Ring C sinks but is light and scratches easily. Which material is it, and why would a camp choose it for a keyring tag?"],key:["A. It is by far the lightest, it is the only one that floats, and it scratches easily, all of which fit plastic. (5-PS1-3)","B is the magnetic steel. The magnet test tells them apart: B is pulled and D is not, even though both are heavy and hard. (5-PS1-3)","The aluminum. It is light enough not to weigh a pocket down and it does not rust, and being soft enough to scratch means a name can be stamped straight into it. (5-PS1-3)"]}]},{id:18,title:"The Sealed Cooler",short:"Weigh In",parts:[{std:["5-PS1-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"using measurements to show that total weight is conserved through heating, cooling, and mixing",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three sealed containers were weighed at breakfast, left in the sun all day, and weighed again at supper. None was opened.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Sealed container</th><th>What happened inside</th><th>Breakfast</th><th>Supper</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>a block of ice, now a pool of water</td><td>1,200 g</td><td>?</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>lake water, now with a crust on the walls and less in the bottom</td><td>840 g</td><td>?</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>water with lemonade powder stirred in until clear</td><td>615 g</td><td>615 g</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Container 1 held ice and now holds water. What does it weigh at supper?","Container 2 has a crust on the walls and less liquid. What does it weigh, and where did the liquid go?","A camper says the cooler must be lighter because the ice is gone. Explain the mistake."],key:["1,200 g. Melting rearranges the particles from solid to liquid without adding or removing any, and nothing left the sealed container. (5-PS1-2)","840 g. Water left the pool as vapor and some minerals were left as a crust, but all of it, vapor included, is still inside the sealed container. (5-PS1-2)","The ice did not go anywhere, it turned into water in the same sealed container. Weight depends on how much matter is there, not on whether it is solid or liquid. (5-PS1-2)"]},{std:["5-PS1-4"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"deciding from evidence whether mixing two substances made a new substance",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three changes seen around the camp in one week.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Change</th><th>What was seen</th></tr>
     <tr><td>logs burned down in the fire ring</td><td>a heap of light gray ash far lighter than the wood, plus smoke and heat; no amount of effort turns the ash back into wood</td></tr>
     <tr><td>lemonade powder stirred into water</td><td>clear sweet drink; left in a dish in the sun, the water went and the powder was left as a sticky layer</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a jug of milk left out of the cooler for a day</td><td>sour smell, thick lumps in a thin liquid, and chilling it again changed nothing</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which two changes made new substances? Give the evidence for each.","Which one is only a mixture, and what is the evidence?","The ash weighs far less than the wood did. Does that mean matter was destroyed? Explain."],key:["The burning logs, because ash, smoke, and heat appeared and the wood cannot be brought back, and the milk left out, because it turned sour and lumpy and cooling it did not reverse that. (5-PS1-4)","The lemonade powder and water. Letting the water go left the powder behind, so it had only been dissolved. (5-PS1-4)","No. Most of the wood's matter left as gases and smoke carried up into the air, which nobody weighed. If you could collect everything, the gases, the smoke, and the ash together would weigh what the wood plus the oxygen did. (5-PS1-4)"]}]},{id:19,title:"Floating Plants",short:"Plant Food",parts:[{std:["5-LS1-1"],n:3,dur:70,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"arguing from measurements that plants get their material for growth from air and water",html:()=>`<p class="big">A camp group scooped a handful of duckweed from the lake into a tub of lake water. The duckweed floats, with no soil of any kind. They dried and weighed a sample at the start and end of the month.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("herb",80)}${F.icon("wave",80)}${F.icon("sun",80)}</div>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th></th><th>Day 1</th><th>Day 30</th></tr>
     <tr><td>duckweed in the tub, dried</td><td>4 g</td><td>60 g</td></tr>
     <tr><td>soil in the tub</td><td>none</td><td>none</td></tr>
     <tr><td>minerals dissolved in the water</td><td>a trace</td><td>a smaller trace</td></tr></table>`,qs:["How much dry weight did the duckweed gain, and how much soil was in the tub?","Where did the new material come from? Name both sources.","A second tub was kept in a dark shed with the same water. The duckweed there lost weight. What does that add to the argument?"],key:["It gained 56 g of dry weight, and there was no soil in the tub at any point. (5-LS1-1)","From air and water. Carbon dioxide from the air and water from the tub are built into the plant, using energy from sunlight. The dissolved minerals supply tiny amounts the plant also needs. (5-LS1-1)","It shows sunlight is needed to build the material. With the same water and the same air but no light, the plant could not build anything and lived off what it had stored until it shrank. Air and water alone are not enough. (5-LS1-1)"]},{std:["5-PS3-1"],n:3,dur:70,dir:"Look at the chain. Answer out loud.",desc:"using a model to trace the energy in an animal's food back to the sun",html:()=>`<p class="big">One path energy takes in the lake.</p>
     <p class="huge" style="font-size:2.2rem;margin:10px 0;text-align:center">${F.icon("sun",46)} &rarr; ${F.icon("herb",46)} &rarr; ${F.icon("fish",46)} &rarr; ${F.icon("eagle",46)}</p>
     <p class="big">Tiny floating plants are eaten by small fish. An osprey catches the fish.</p>`,qs:["Trace the energy in the osprey's meal back to its source.","The tiny floating plants live near the surface of the lake. Explain why, in terms of energy.","A camper says the osprey gets its energy from the lake. Improve that answer."],key:["From the fish, which got it from the tiny floating plants, which captured energy from sunlight and stored it in the food they made. (5-PS3-1)","They need sunlight to make their food, and sunlight only reaches the upper layer of the water. Deeper down there is too little light to live on. (5-PS3-1)","The lake is where the osprey hunts, not where the energy comes from. The energy came from sunlight, captured by floating plants, passed to the fish, and then to the osprey. (5-PS3-1)"]}]},{id:20,title:"The Lake Model",short:"Matter Cycle",parts:[{std:["5-LS2-1"],n:4,dur:90,dir:"Look at the model. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing with a model how matter moves among plants, animals, decomposers, and the environment",html:()=>`<p class="big">A model of the lake at the camp.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>From</th><th>To</th><th>How</th></tr>
     <tr><td>water, air, and lake bed</td><td>floating plants and weed</td><td>they take in carbon dioxide, water, and minerals</td></tr>
     <tr><td>floating plants and weed</td><td>insect larvae and small fish</td><td>eaten</td></tr>
     <tr><td>insect larvae and small fish</td><td>bigger fish, frogs, and herons</td><td>eaten</td></tr>
     <tr><td>everything that dies, plus waste</td><td>bacteria and fungi on the lake bed</td><td>broken down</td></tr>
     <tr><td>bacteria and fungi</td><td>water, air, and lake bed</td><td>minerals and gases released</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("fish",70)}${F.icon("frog",70)}${F.icon("mushroom",70)}${F.icon("herb",70)}</div>`,qs:["Name the decomposers in the model and say where they work.","A heron eats a fish and flies off to nest in a tree two miles away. What does that do to the matter in this model?","One summer a great deal of fertilizer washes into the lake. The floating plants grow enormously, then die all at once and the water goes low in oxygen. Use the model to explain that.","Trace one mineral from a dead leaf on the lake bed into a small fish."],key:["Bacteria and fungi, working on the lake bed on everything that dies and on waste. (5-LS2-1)","It carries matter out of the lake. Part of the fish ends up in the heron and later in the tree's soil through its waste, so a model of only the lake does not capture everything. Matter moves between places, not just around one. (5-LS2-1)","Extra minerals let the first row run far faster, so plants grow enormously. When they die, the decomposers in the fourth row work hard on all that material, and breaking it down uses up the oxygen in the water, which is why the lake runs low and other living things suffer. (5-LS2-1)","Bacteria and fungi break the leaf down and release the mineral into the water, floating plants take it up and build it into themselves, and a small fish eats them. (5-LS2-1)"]}]},{id:21,title:"Off the Dock",short:"Down",parts:[{std:["5-PS2-1"],n:3,dur:70,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"arguing that the gravitational force Earth exerts on objects is directed down",html:()=>`<p class="big">A paddle slips off the dock. A plumb line hangs beside the camp flagpole while it is being set upright.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("canoe",80)}${F.icon("flag",80)}${F.icon("wave",80)}</div>`,qs:["Which way does the dropped paddle go, and which way does the plumb line point?","A camper floats on her back in the lake and does not sink. Is Earth still pulling her down? Explain.","A ball thrown upward slows, stops, and comes back. Which way was the pull acting during the whole throw, including on the way up?"],key:["Both go straight down, the paddle toward the water and the plumb line toward the ground. (5-PS2-1)","Yes. The pull is still straight down, and the water pushes up on her with an equal force, so she neither rises nor sinks. The pull did not stop; it was balanced. (5-PS2-1)","Straight down the whole time. On the way up the downward pull slowed the ball, at the top it had stopped the upward motion, and after that the same downward pull brought it back. (5-PS2-1)"]},{std:["5-ESS2-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the story. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing with an example how the geosphere, biosphere, hydrosphere, and atmosphere interact",html:()=>`<p class="big">The Earth system has four parts. The geosphere is rock and soil. The biosphere is living things. The hydrosphere is all the water. The atmosphere is air.</p>
     <p class="big">A hot, still fortnight warms the top of the lake. Warm water holds less dissolved oxygen, so the fish move deeper to find cooler water. Shallow bays drop several centimeters as water leaves into the dry air, and the exposed mud cracks. In the reed beds, the reeds keep the bank from crumbling and shade the water just behind them, which stays cooler.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("thermometer",72)}${F.icon("fish",72)}${F.icon("herb",72)}${F.icon("sun",72)}</div>`,qs:["Warm air warms the lake and the warm water holds less oxygen. Name the two spheres in that sentence and then the sphere affected next.","Shallow bays drop and the exposed mud cracks. Which spheres interacted, and in which order?","The reeds do two different jobs in the story. Name both and say which spheres each one connects."],key:["The atmosphere warms the hydrosphere, and then the biosphere is affected, because the fish have to move to find water with enough oxygen. (5-ESS2-1)","The atmosphere took water out of the hydrosphere by evaporation, which then exposed the geosphere, the mud, which dried and cracked. (5-ESS2-1)","They hold the bank together, which is the biosphere holding the geosphere in place against the hydrosphere, and they shade the water, which is the biosphere affecting the hydrosphere's temperature by blocking sunlight. (5-ESS2-1)"]}]},{id:22,title:"Summer Sky",short:"Sky",parts:[{std:["5-ESS1-1"],n:3,dur:70,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"arguing that the sun looks brightest because of how near it is, not because it gives off the most light",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three stars of the summer sky, with the light each gives off and its distance.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Star</th><th>Light it gives off</th><th>Distance from Earth</th></tr>
     <tr><td>the sun</td><td>1 unit</td><td>8 light-minutes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Vega</td><td>about 40 units</td><td>25 light-years</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Antares</td><td>tens of thousands of units</td><td>about 550 light-years</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("sun",76)}${F.icon("star",76)}${F.icon("tent",76)}</div>`,qs:["Which star on the chart gives off the least light? Which looks brightest from the camp?","Antares gives off tens of thousands of times more light than Vega but looks about as bright. Explain using the distances.","Two campers see two stars that look the same brightness and conclude they are the same kind of star. What is missing from that reasoning?"],key:["The sun gives off the least of the three. The sun also looks far and away the brightest. (5-ESS1-1)","Antares is about 550 light-years away and Vega is 25, so Antares is more than twenty times further. Light spreads out as it travels, and that difference very nearly cancels out Antares giving off so much more light. (5-ESS1-1)","The distance. Two stars can look identical from here and be completely different, one modest and close and the other enormous and far away. You need the distance before you can say anything about the star itself. (5-ESS1-1)"]},{std:["5-ESS1-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"finding patterns in graphed data about shadows, day length, and the night sky",html:()=>`<p class="big">A camp counsellor measured the shadow of the same 150 cm post at the same four times of day, once in June and once in October.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Time</th><th>June shadow</th><th>October shadow</th></tr>
     <tr><td>7 a.m.</td><td>230 cm</td><td>420 cm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>10 a.m.</td><td>85 cm</td><td>175 cm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1 p.m.</td><td>60 cm</td><td>150 cm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4 p.m.</td><td>175 cm</td><td>390 cm</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">June daylight at the camp: 15 hours. October daylight: 11 hours.</p>`,qs:["Describe the pattern within a single day, using either column.","Compare the two columns. What is true of every October shadow, and how does that fit the daylight figures?","At 10 p.m. in June the camp can see a bright group of three stars high overhead. In October at 10 p.m. they are low in the west. What is causing that?"],key:["The shadow is long early, shortest in the middle of the day, and long again in the afternoon. It shortens toward midday and lengthens after. (5-ESS1-2)","Every October shadow is longer than the June shadow at the same time of day. That fits the shorter October day: the midday sun does not climb as high, so it casts longer shadows and spends fewer hours above the horizon. (5-ESS1-2)","Earth travels around the sun over the year, so at the same hour of night we are facing a different direction in space, and stars that were overhead in June have shifted westward by October. (5-ESS1-2)"]}]},{id:23,title:"The Lake and All the Water",short:"Water",parts:[{std:["5-ESS2-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the charts. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing the amounts and percentages of water and fresh water in Earth's reservoirs",html:()=>`<p class="big">All of Earth's water, then the fresh part on its own.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>All water</th><th>Share</th></tr>
     <tr><td>salty ocean</td><td>about 97%</td></tr>
     <tr><td>fresh water</td><td>about 3%</td></tr></table>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>The fresh water</th><th>Share of the fresh part</th></tr>
     <tr><td>ice and glaciers</td><td>about 69%</td></tr>
     <tr><td>groundwater</td><td>about 30%</td></tr>
     <tr><td>lakes</td><td>under 1%</td></tr>
     <tr><td>rivers</td><td>a tiny fraction of 1%</td></tr></table>`,qs:["All the lakes in the world together are under 1% of the fresh water. Work out roughly what share of all Earth's water that is.","Lakes hold more than rivers, yet more cities sit on rivers than on lakes. Suggest why.","A camper says the lake will never run out because there is so much water on Earth. Use the charts to answer that."],key:["Under 1% of about 3%, which is well under one part in a hundred of one part in thirty, so roughly a hundredth of a percent of all Earth's water. Any answer showing the two shares multiplied together counts. (5-ESS2-2)","A river brings water to you and carries waste and boats away, and it keeps being refilled. A lake is a store in one place. Flow is more useful to a city than a store, which is why settlements line rivers. (5-ESS2-2)","Almost all of Earth's water is salty ocean, and most of the fresh water is frozen or deep underground. The water this lake can draw on is a tiny fraction of a tiny fraction, and it depends on local rain and snowmelt, which can fall short. (5-ESS2-2)"]},{std:["5-ESS3-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the two reports. Answer out loud.",desc:"combining information about ways communities use science to protect resources and the environment",html:()=>`<p class="big">Two lake towns worked to keep their water healthy.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Town</th><th>What they did</th><th>Result after three years</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Clearwater</td><td>banned lawn fertilizer containing phosphorus within 100 m of the shore and planted a buffer of native plants along the bank</td><td>summer algae blooms stopped, and the water was clear to 3 m instead of 1 m</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Stonebridge</td><td>built a boat wash station at the ramp and asked every trailer to be rinsed before launching</td><td>no new invasive weed found in the lake, while two nearby lakes were taken over by it</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Why would stopping phosphorus fertilizer near the shore stop algae blooms?","A boat wash station seems like a small thing. Explain how it kept an invasive weed out of a whole lake.","Combine the two reports into a plan for a third lake town with algae blooms and a busy boat ramp."],key:["Phosphorus washing off lawns into the lake is a mineral that algae need, and extra of it lets them grow enormously. Keeping it out of the water removes the thing that was feeding the bloom, and the plant buffer traps more of what still runs off. (5-ESS3-1)","Invasive weeds spread as fragments caught on trailers and hulls, moving from lake to lake with the boats. Rinsing removes the fragments before launch, so the one route into the lake is closed. (5-ESS3-1)","Ban phosphorus fertilizer near the shore and plant a native buffer along the bank, and build a boat wash station at the ramp with rinsing before launch. The plan has to draw on each report. (5-ESS3-1)"]}]},{id:24,title:"Design a Dock Shade",short:"Design It",parts:[{std:["3-5-ETS1-1"],n:3,dur:60,dir:"Listen, then answer out loud.",desc:"defining a design problem with criteria for success and constraints",read:"The swim dock has no shade, and by midday the boards are too hot to sit on and swimmers crowd into the one patch of shade from a tree on the bank. The camp wants something that shades at least half the dock through the middle of the day. It must not block the ladder or the swim lanes, it must come down in ten minutes when a storm is forecast, and it can only be built from the poles, rope, and old sailcloth already in the boathouse.",qs:["Say the problem in one sentence.","Name three things the finished shade must do to count as a success.","Name two limits the camp has to work inside."],key:["The swim dock has no shade, so the boards get too hot to sit on and everyone crowds into one small shaded spot. The camp needs shade over the dock. (3-5-ETS1-1)","Any three criteria from the story: shades at least half the dock in the middle of the day, does not block the ladder or the swim lanes, and comes down in ten minutes. (3-5-ETS1-1)","Any two constraints: only poles, rope, and old sailcloth from the boathouse, and it must be removable in ten minutes. Accept the ten minute rule as either a criterion or a constraint if the student explains. (3-5-ETS1-1)"]},{std:["3-5-ETS1-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"comparing possible solutions against the criteria and constraints",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three designs, each measured at noon on the same sunny day.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Design</th><th>Dock shaded at noon</th><th>Blocks ladder or lanes?</th><th>Time to take down</th><th>Boathouse materials only?</th></tr>
     <tr><td>L: one sail stretched between four poles over the middle</td><td>55%</td><td>no</td><td>8 min</td><td>yes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>M: two sails covering the whole dock, poles at every corner</td><td>95%</td><td>yes, a pole stands in the lane</td><td>20 min</td><td>yes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>N: a bought cantilever umbrella bolted to the dock</td><td>60%</td><td>no</td><td>cannot be taken down</td><td>no</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which design shades the most? Name the two criteria it fails.","Design N shades slightly more than L. Name the two reasons it is still ruled out.","Which design would you recommend, and list every criterion and limit it satisfies."],key:["M shades 95%, but a pole stands in a swim lane and it takes twenty minutes to take down rather than ten. (3-5-ETS1-2)","It cannot be taken down at all when a storm is forecast, and it has to be bought rather than built from boathouse materials. (3-5-ETS1-2)","L. It shades 55% of the dock, which meets the at least half requirement, it blocks neither the ladder nor the lanes, it comes down in 8 minutes, and it uses only a sail, poles, and rope from the boathouse. (3-5-ETS1-2)"]},{std:["3-5-ETS1-3"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the test plan. Answer out loud.",desc:"planning a fair test with controlled variables and naming the failure points to improve",html:()=>`<p class="big">The camp tested how high to set the sail.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th></th><th>Test 1</th><th>Test 2</th></tr>
     <tr><td>sail height above the dock</td><td>2.0 m</td><td>2.8 m</td></tr>
     <tr><td>sail size</td><td>4 m by 4 m</td><td>4 m by 4 m</td></tr>
     <tr><td>time measured</td><td>noon, clear sky</td><td>noon, clear sky</td></tr>
     <tr><td>dock shaded</td><td>55%</td><td>48%</td></tr>
     <tr><td>how it felt underneath</td><td>shaded but stuffy, no breeze</td><td>cooler, good breeze</td></tr>
     <tr><td>what went wrong</td><td>tall swimmers knocked their heads on the low corner</td><td>the sail lifted and flapped hard in a gust</td></tr></table>`,qs:["What was held the same, and what was changed? Why does that make the test fair?","Test 2 shades less but feels better. Explain how a design can be worse on the measurement and still be the better choice.","Name one change that would fix the failure in each test."],key:["The sail size and the time and sky conditions were held the same, and only the height changed, so the difference in shade can be traced to the height. (3-5-ETS1-3)","The point was a dock people can use comfortably. Shade is one criterion and airflow matters too, so losing 7% of the shaded area in exchange for a cooler, breezier dock may serve the real goal better. A measurement only counts against the criteria that matter. (3-5-ETS1-3)","Test 1 was too low at the corners, so raise the corners, pull the sail tighter so it does not sag, or set the poles further out. Test 2 lifted in a gust, so add more tie-down lines, cut a vent in the sail, or use a less steeply angled set. Any fix tied to the stated failure counts. (3-5-ETS1-3)"]}]},{id:25,title:"Living With the Climate",short:"Climate",parts:[{std:["HSS-5.1"],n:4,dur:110,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing how pre-Columbian nations adapted to very different climates",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three regions with very different problems to solve, and what nations there worked out.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Region</th><th>The problem</th><th>What people did about it</th></tr>
     <tr><td>desert Southwest</td><td>barely any rain, and what falls comes in short bursts</td><td>channels and small dams to catch and spread flood water, fields placed where water collects, and crops bred for dry ground</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Great Plains</td><td>treeless grassland and a food supply that moves</td><td>portable homes, dried and pounded meat that keeps for months, and setting grass fires on purpose to bring fresh growth and draw herds</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Pacific Northwest coast</td><td>huge food supply, but only for a few weeks a year</td><td>smoking and drying on a large scale, big storage buildings, and plank houses that last for generations</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Setting grass fires on purpose sounds destructive. Explain why nations of the Plains did it.","The Pacific Northwest had plenty of food. Explain why storage was still the hardest problem to solve there.","In the desert Southwest, where would you put a field, and why?","Pick two regions and explain how the same goal, having food all year, led to completely different solutions."],key:["Burning old grass brings fresh green growth the following season, and buffalo graze where the new grass is. It was a way of managing the grassland so the herds came within reach. Partial: naming the fresh grass with no link to where the herds graze is approaching. (HSS-5.1)","The salmon ran for only a few weeks a year, so a year's food had to be caught and preserved in that short window. Catching it was the easy part; keeping it edible for ten more months was the problem. Partial: saying they had a lot of fish with nothing said about the short run is approaching. (HSS-5.1)","Where flood water collects or can be led, at the foot of a slope or along a channel dug from a wash, so the short bursts of rain soak the field rather than running past it. Partial: saying near water with no account of leading or collecting the flood is approaching. (HSS-5.1)","Any supported pair. For example on the Plains people followed the moving food and carried preserved meat with them, while on the coast people stayed put and built large stores and lasting houses, because their food came to one place at one time of year. Partial: two regions named with no account of the different solutions is approaching. (HSS-5.1)"]}]},{id:26,title:"Maps That Got Better",short:"Maps",parts:[{std:["HSS-5.2"],n:4,dur:100,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"tracing how early explorations changed European maps of the Americas and what they got wrong",html:()=>`<p class="big">Each voyage sent information home, and the maps changed.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Roughly when</th><th>What European maps showed</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1490</td><td>Europe, Africa, and Asia, with open ocean where the Americas are</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1500</td><td>scattered islands in the west, believed to lie off the coast of Asia</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1520</td><td>a long continuous coast running far north and south, clearly not Asia</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1600</td><td>both American coastlines fairly accurate, the interior largely blank or guessed at</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1700</td><td>major rivers and lakes added, from the routes of inland expeditions and from information given by American Indian nations</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("map",76)}${F.icon("globe",76)}${F.icon("compass",76)}</div>`,qs:["What changed on the maps between 1500 and 1520, and which voyages would have caused that?","In 1600 the coasts were good and the interior was blank. Explain why those two parts of the map improved at different rates.","The 1700 row credits information given by American Indian nations. Why would that be the only way to map the interior?","Columbus died believing he had reached Asia. Looking at the 1500 row, explain how that belief was possible."],key:["Scattered islands became one long continuous coast that clearly was not Asia. Voyages along the coast of South and North America in those decades, including Magellan's expedition reaching the far south, showed the land ran on and on. Partial: naming the change with no voyage that would have caused it is approaching. (HSS-5.2)","Ships sailed the coasts, so coastlines were measured and recorded voyage after voyage. Almost no European had crossed the interior, so there was nothing to draw it from. A map is only as good as where people have been and what they were told. Partial: one of the two parts explained is approaching. (HSS-5.2)","The nations living there knew the rivers, lakes, portages, and distances, having used them for generations. Expeditions depended on being guided and told, and much of what reached European maps came that way. Partial: saying they helped with nothing said about the knowledge they held is approaching. (HSS-5.2)","The maps of 1500 showed only scattered islands in the west and nobody knew how far the Pacific stretched, so islands off the Asian coast was a reasonable reading of the evidence he had. He had no way to see the continent behind them. Partial: calling him mistaken with no account of the evidence he had is approaching. (HSS-5.2)"]},{std:["HSS-5.3"],n:4,dur:100,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing the range of ways American Indian nations responded to settlement",html:()=>`<p class="big">Nations facing settlement had choices to make, and different nations chose differently at different times.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Response</th><th>Example</th></tr>
     <tr><td>negotiate and sign agreements</td><td>many nations signed treaties setting boundaries, trade terms, and payments</td></tr>
     <tr><td>ally with one European power against another</td><td>nations took sides in the wars between Britain and France for their own reasons</td></tr>
     <tr><td>move to land further from settlement</td><td>whole nations relocated, sometimes onto land another nation already used</td></tr>
     <tr><td>adopt what was useful and keep their own government</td><td>the Cherokee developed a written alphabet, newspapers, and a written constitution of their own</td></tr>
     <tr><td>resist by force</td><td>nations fought to hold territory when agreements failed</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Name two different responses on the chart and say what each one was trying to achieve.","Moving away sometimes meant moving onto land another nation used. Explain the problem that created.","The Cherokee built a written alphabet, newspapers, and a constitution. Why would a nation do that, and what does it show?","These responses are all different. What does that tell you about treating American Indian nations as one group with one story?"],key:["Any two. Negotiating aimed to set a firm boundary and get terms in writing. Allying aimed to gain support and limit settlement. Moving aimed to get out of the way of pressure. Adopting what was useful aimed to meet settlers on their own terms while keeping self-government. Resisting aimed to hold territory. Partial: two responses named with no aim for either is approaching. (HSS-5.3)","It pushed the pressure on to another nation, who then had less land and a new neighbor they had not chosen, which could set two nations against each other over ground that had been enough for one. Partial: saying they had to move again with nothing said about the other nation is approaching. (HSS-5.3)","To deal with the United States on its own terms: to keep records, argue in print, petition courts, and show they were a government. It shows nations adapted deliberately and were not simply overtaken, and that adapting did not save their land either. Partial: one of the reason and what it shows given is approaching. (HSS-5.3)","That it is wrong. Hundreds of separate nations made their own decisions in their own situations, and treating them as one group hides both the variety of what they did and the fact that they were acting, not just being acted upon. Partial: saying they were all different with nothing said about nations acting rather than only being acted upon is approaching. (HSS-5.3)"]}]},{id:27,title:"Making a Living, Making a Case",short:"Colonies",parts:[{std:["HSS-5.4"],n:4,dur:135,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing how colonists earned a living and how colonial money and trade worked",html:()=>`<p class="big">A colonial household rarely earned wages the way people do now.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>How people got by</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
     <tr><td>family farming</td><td>most households grew most of what they ate and sold or traded the surplus</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a trade learned by apprenticeship</td><td>blacksmith, cooper, printer, shoemaker, shipwright; often paid partly in goods</td></tr>
     <tr><td>barter and book credit</td><td>coins were scarce, so a shopkeeper kept a ledger of who owed what and accounts were settled in goods or labor</td></tr>
     <tr><td>colonial paper money and foreign coins</td><td>colonies printed their own notes; Spanish silver dollars circulated widely because British coin was short</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the minister's rate</td><td>a New England town voted a tax to pay the minister of its Puritan congregation, and much of it came in as grain, firewood or a few days' labor rather than coin; in Virginia and several other colonies a parish tax supported the established Anglican minister the same way</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Coins were scarce. Explain how a shopkeeper could still do business all year.","A New England town paid the minister of its congregation partly in grain and firewood rather than money. Name one advantage and one difficulty of being paid that way.","Spanish silver coins circulated widely in British colonies. What does that tell you about the colonial money supply?","Colonies printed their own paper money. Name one risk that carries."],key:["Through barter and book credit. Goods and labor changed hands and the shopkeeper wrote down who owed what, settling up later in goods, work, or whatever coin turned up. Partial: naming barter with nothing said about written credit is approaching. (HSS-5.4)","An advantage is that it works when coin is scarce, and the grain and firewood are exactly what the household needs to eat and stay warm. The difficulty is that you cannot easily pass them on, store them, or use them to pay a debt that must be settled in money, and the amount depends on how good the harvest was. Partial: one of the advantage and the difficulty given is approaching. (HSS-5.4)","That Britain did not supply enough coin for the colonies' trade, so people used whatever reliable silver they could get hold of. A shortage of official money pushed foreign coin into everyday use. Partial: noting the Spanish coin with nothing said about the shortage is approaching. (HSS-5.4)","If too much is printed it loses value and prices rise, so wages and savings buy less. That happened badly during the Revolution. Partial: saying paper money is risky with no account of what happens to prices is approaching. (HSS-5.4)"]},{std:["HSS-5.5"],n:4,dur:100,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"sorting the causes of the American Revolution by kind",html:()=>`<p class="big">Historians sort causes into kinds as well as by date.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Cause</th><th>Economic, political, or about ideas?</th></tr>
     <tr><td>taxes on printed paper, glass, and tea</td><td>?</td></tr>
     <tr><td>colonists had no elected members in Parliament</td><td>?</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the belief that people hold rights no government may take away</td><td>?</td></tr>
     <tr><td>rules that certain goods could only be shipped to Britain</td><td>?</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Parliament stripping powers from the Massachusetts assembly</td><td>?</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Sort the five causes into economic, political, and about ideas.","One of them could fairly be put in two columns. Pick one and argue for both placements.","Which kind of cause do you think was strongest, and why?","Why would a historian bother sorting causes by kind at all?"],key:["Economic: taxes on goods, and the shipping rules. Political: no elected members in Parliament, and stripping powers from the Massachusetts assembly. About ideas: the belief in rights no government may take away. Partial: three or four of the five sorted right is approaching. (HSS-5.5)","Any supported answer. The taxes are economic because they cost money and political because the objection was about who had the right to impose them. No representation is political and also about ideas, since it rests on a belief about consent. Partial: a cause named with only one placement argued is approaching. (HSS-5.5)","Any supported answer. A case for ideas: the sums were small and taxes were sometimes repealed, yet the dispute went on, which suggests the principle mattered more. A case for political: losing control of their own assemblies was the concrete loss. The reason is what counts. Partial: a kind chosen with no reason is approaching. (HSS-5.5)","It stops a single kind of explanation taking over. Looking only at money misses why small taxes caused such anger; looking only at ideas misses whose livelihoods were affected. Sorting them keeps all of it in view. Partial: saying it is tidier with no account of what a single explanation would miss is approaching. (HSS-5.5)"]},{std:["HSS-5.6"],n:4,dur:110,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing the consequences of the Revolution for groups other than the winners",html:()=>`<p class="big">The war ended in 1783. What followed was very different depending on who you were.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Group</th><th>What independence meant for them</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Loyalists</td><td>tens of thousands left for Canada, Britain, or the Caribbean, many losing their land and businesses; those who stayed faced suspicion</td></tr>
     <tr><td>American Indian nations</td><td>Britain handed their land to the United States in a treaty they were not part of, and settlement pressure increased sharply</td></tr>
     <tr><td>enslaved people</td><td>slavery ended in several northern states over the following decades and expanded in the South; thousands who had joined the British left with them as free people</td></tr>
     <tr><td>women</td><td>no change in the right to vote or own property in most states, though arguments about education and rights grew louder</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Name two groups for whom the end of the war was not an improvement, and say why in each case.","Britain gave away land in the 1783 treaty that it did not occupy. Explain the problem with that.","Slavery ended in parts of the North and expanded in the South over the same decades. What does that tell you about the new country?","Pick one group on the chart and say what they might have argued they were owed."],key:["Any two with reasons. Loyalists lost property and often their homes and country. American Indian nations had their land signed away by a power that did not live on it and faced far greater settlement pressure. Partial: two groups named with no reason for either is approaching. (HSS-5.6)","The land was the territory of American Indian nations who had not agreed to anything and were not at the talks. Two European powers were trading away other people's homes on paper. Partial: noting Britain did not live there with nothing said about the nations who did is approaching. (HSS-5.6)","That it was not one country with one view. States made their own laws, and the same document that said all men are created equal governed a country where slavery grew, which set up a conflict that was not settled for another eighty years. Partial: noting the difference between North and South with nothing said about the conflict it set up is approaching. (HSS-5.6)","Any supported answer. African American soldiers were promised freedom for serving and could argue it should be honored. Women who ran farms, made supplies, and kept the economy going could argue for education and a political voice. Nations who allied with the Americans could argue their boundaries should be respected. Partial: a group named with nothing they could argue they were owed is approaching. (HSS-5.6)"]}]},{id:28,title:"The Convention and the Trail",short:"New Nation",parts:[{std:["HSS-5.7"],n:4,dur:110,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing the Constitutional Convention, who took part, and how it worked",html:()=>`<p class="big">Fifty-five delegates met in Philadelphia from May to September 1787.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Fact</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
     <tr><td>who attended</td><td>delegates from twelve states; Rhode Island sent none</td></tr>
     <tr><td>who did not</td><td>no women, no Black people, no American Indian people, and nobody without property</td></tr>
     <tr><td>how it was run</td><td>the windows were kept shut and the debates kept secret, so delegates could change their minds without being held to earlier positions</td></tr>
     <tr><td>what they were sent to do</td><td>officially, to amend the Articles of Confederation; what they did instead was write an entirely new document</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the record</td><td>James Madison's notes are the main source for what was said</td></tr></table>`,qs:["The delegates were sent to amend the Articles and wrote a new document instead. Why would that be controversial?","Name one advantage and one disadvantage of keeping the debates secret.","Look at who was not there. How might the document have differed if those groups had been represented?","Madison's notes are the main record. Name one reason a historian treats a single person's notes with care."],key:["They went beyond what they had been authorized to do. Critics could say the whole document was illegitimate, and that is part of why ratification by the states, rather than by Congress alone, mattered so much. Partial: noting they wrote something new with nothing said about authority or ratification is approaching. (HSS-5.7)","An advantage is that delegates could argue, concede, and change their minds without being attacked at home for it, which made compromise possible. A disadvantage is that the public had no idea what was being decided in their name until it was finished. Partial: one of the advantage and the disadvantage given is approaching. (HSS-5.7)","Any thoughtful answer. Representation for enslaved people would have made the compromises over slavery impossible to write as they were; representation for women might have put property and voting rights in the document; representation for American Indian nations would have raised land and treaty questions the document left to Congress. Partial: naming who was missing with no account of how the document might differ is approaching. (HSS-5.7)","One person chooses what to write down, may summarize or sharpen what others said, and may have his own case to make. Madison was a central participant, not a neutral observer, and he edited his notes later. Partial: saying one person could be wrong with no account of selection or his own case is approaching. (HSS-5.7)"]},{std:["HSS-5.8"],n:4,dur:100,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining the overland trails west and the cost of making the journey",html:()=>`<p class="big">From the 1840s, families crossed overland to Oregon and California. The journey took most of a year's savings and most of a summer.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Item</th><th>Rough figure</th></tr>
     <tr><td>distance from Missouri to Oregon</td><td>about 2,000 miles</td></tr>
     <tr><td>time on the trail</td><td>four to six months, walking beside the wagon</td></tr>
     <tr><td>cost to outfit one family</td><td>several hundred dollars, often a year or more of earnings</td></tr>
     <tr><td>what the wagon carried</td><td>mostly food, around a ton of flour, bacon, beans, coffee, and sugar, plus tools and a few possessions</td></tr>
     <tr><td>when they had to leave</td><td>after spring grass for the animals, but early enough to cross the mountains before snow</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Explain why the departure date was so tightly fixed.","The wagon was mostly food, not furniture. Explain why, using the chart.","The outfit cost a year of earnings. What does that tell you about who could and could not go?","Name two things about the route that geography decided."],key:["They had to wait for spring grass so the oxen could feed along the way, and they had to reach the mountain passes before the autumn snow closed them. That left a window of only a few weeks to start. Partial: one of the spring grass and the autumn snow named is approaching. (HSS-5.8)","There was nowhere to buy food for 2,000 miles and four to six months, so a family had to carry every meal. A ton of food leaves very little room or hauling capacity for anything else. Partial: saying they needed food with nothing said about the distance or the months is approaching. (HSS-5.8)","The poorest families could not go at all, since the outfit alone cost more than they could save. Those who went were mostly families with some land or savings to sell, which shaped who ended up out west. Partial: saying it was expensive with nothing said about who could and could not go is approaching. (HSS-5.8)","Any two: the trail followed river valleys such as the Platte because the animals needed water and grass, and it funnelled through South Pass because that was the crossing wagons could manage. Mountains and deserts set the timing and the order of the stages. Partial: one thing named is approaching. (HSS-5.8)"]},{std:["HSS-5.9"],n:5,dur:90,dir:"Answer out loud. A map is fine to look at.",desc:"locating Great Lakes states and naming their capitals",html:()=>`<div class="figure">${F.icon("wave",96)}${F.icon("map",96)}</div>
     <p class="big">Questions about the Great Lakes region.</p>`,qs:["Name the five Great Lakes.","What is the capital of Michigan, and what is the capital of Wisconsin?","Which Great Lake lies entirely within the United States, and which state is it named for?","What is the capital of Minnesota, and which large city sits beside it?","How many states are there in all, and name three that touch Lake Michigan."],key:["Superior, Michigan, Huron, Erie, and Ontario. Partial: three or four of the five named is approaching. (HSS-5.9)","Lansing, Michigan and Madison, Wisconsin. Note if the student names Detroit or Milwaukee, which is the usual slip. Partial: one of the two capitals right is approaching. (HSS-5.9)","Lake Michigan lies entirely within the United States, and it shares its name with the state of Michigan. Partial: the lake named with no state named is approaching. (HSS-5.9)","Saint Paul, Minnesota, with Minneapolis beside it. Partial: the capital right with no second city named is approaching. (HSS-5.9)","50 states. Wisconsin, Illinois, Indiana, and Michigan all touch Lake Michigan. Partial: the count right with two of the three states named is approaching. (HSS-5.9)"]}]}];const WC=FLUENCY.split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean).length;const readPart=STOPS[1].parts[0];readPart.n=WC;readPart.key[0]=readPart.key[0].replace("__WC__",WC);readPart.desc=`a ${WC}-word grade-level passage read aloud`;const LEVELUPS={3:{id:"L3",lv:true,title:"Level Up: Cite the Evidence",std:["RL.6.1"],needs:["RL.5.1"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Use the story and cite your evidence.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>What kind of brother is Ignatius? Cite two details from the story that support your answer.</li><li>The story never says that the logbook turned out to be worth keeping. What evidence in the story supports that idea?</li></ol>${F.passage(LIST,LIST_TITLE,true)}`,key:["Stubborn at first and then quietly fair. Any two cited details: he called the book a waste of a morning in the middle of July, he said nobody reads it, he said nothing at all after the green paddle was found, and on the last morning he took the book down himself and wrote the time without being asked. Two cited details are needed. (RL.6.1, cites evidence to support an analysis)","A family asked about a green paddle, Esperanza turned back eleven pages and found the exact line about canoe four, and the paddle was in the rack where she had put it. The record answered a real question that nobody could have answered from memory. (RL.6.1, inference with cited evidence)"]},4:{id:"L4",lv:true,title:"Level Up: Evidence in the Article",std:["RI.6.1"],needs:["RI.5.1"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Cite evidence from the texts.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>The texts never use the word trapped. Cite two details that show the bottom layer is shut away from the rest of the lake for months at a time.</li><li>Do the texts claim that the layers last all year? Cite the evidence for your answer.</li></ol>${textBlock(S1,S2,S3)}`,key:["Any two: wind can stir the top layer but cannot mix across the thin band in the middle; for three or four months the bottom and the top barely trade any water; and once the layers set up, the bottom layer is cut off from the air. Two cited details are needed. (RI.6.1, cites evidence to support an analysis)","No. Text 1 says the layers hold for three or four months, and Text 3 says the profile shows the layers forming in June and breaking down again in the fall, when the surface cools and the first strong wind mixes the whole lake. (RI.6.1, cites evidence)"]},11:{id:"L11",lv:true,title:"Level Up: Power Up",std:["6.EE.A.1"],needs:["5.OA.A.1"],n:3,dir:"Solve on your paper. Then tell your teacher your answers.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>4<sup>2</sup> = ?</li><li>3<sup>3</sup> − 7 = ?</li><li>Write 5 × 5 × 5 using an exponent. Then find its value.</li></ol>`,key:["16, because 4 × 4 = 16. (6.EE.A.1, whole number exponents)","20, because 3 × 3 × 3 = 27 and 27 − 7 = 20. (6.EE.A.1)","5 cubed, written with a small 3 above the 5, which equals 125. (6.EE.A.1)"]},13:{id:"L13",lv:true,title:"Level Up: Divide the Fractions",std:["6.NS.A.1"],needs:["5.NF.B.3"],n:3,dir:"Solve on your paper. Then tell your teacher your answers.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>3/4 ÷ 1/8 = ?</li><li>How many water bottles that each hold 2/3 of a liter can be filled from 6 liters?</li><li>4 campers share 2/5 of a kilogram of trail mix equally. How much does each one get?</li></ol>`,key:["6. 3/4 ÷ 1/8 = 3/4 × 8/1 = 24/4 = 6. (6.NS.A.1)","9 bottles. 6 ÷ 2/3 = 6 × 3/2 = 18/2 = 9. (6.NS.A.1)","1/10 of a kilogram. 2/5 ÷ 4 = 2/5 × 1/4 = 2/20 = 1/10. (6.NS.A.1)"]},14:{id:"L14",lv:true,title:"Level Up: Fractional Edges",std:["6.G.A.2"],needs:["5.MD.C.5"],n:2,dir:"Solve on your paper and hold it up.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>A folded tarp box is 3/4 foot long, 1/2 foot wide, and 2/3 foot tall. What is its volume?</li><li>A box is 1 1/2 inches by 2 inches by 3 inches. What is its volume?</li></ol>`,key:["1/4 of a cubic foot. 3/4 × 1/2 × 2/3 = 6/24 = 1/4. (6.G.A.2, volume with fractional edge lengths)","9 cubic inches. 3/2 × 2 × 3 = 18/2 = 9. (6.G.A.2)"]},15:{id:"L15",lv:true,title:"Level Up: Four Quadrants",std:["6.NS.C.6"],needs:["5.G.A.1"],n:3,dir:"Look at the grid. Answer out loud.",html:()=>`<div class="figure">${F.coordGrid({max:5,quad4:true,size:380,points:[[-4,1,"M"],[3,-2,"N"]]})}</div><ol class="qlist"><li>What are the coordinates of point M?</li><li>What are the coordinates of point N?</li><li>Point P is at (−1, −5). Which two moves take you from the origin to P?</li></ol>`,key:["Negative 4, 1. Written (−4, 1). (6.NS.C.6, points in all four quadrants)","3, negative 2. Written (3, −2). (6.NS.C.6)","1 unit to the left, then 5 units down. (6.NS.C.6)"]}};const PASSAGE_STD=["RL.5.1","RL.5.4","RI.5.1","RI.5.9","W.5.8","W.5.2","RL.6.1","RI.6.1"];const MULT_N=12;let multSel=[0,0],pvVal="";function multHtml(){let h=`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="mult"><tr><th>×</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++)h+=`<th data-r="0" data-c="${c}" class="${multSel[1]===c?"hl":""}">${c}</th>`;h+="</tr>";for(let r=1;r<=MULT_N;r++){h+=`<tr><th data-r="${r}" data-c="0" class="${multSel[0]===r?"hl":""}">${r}</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++){const hr=multSel[0]===r,hc=multSel[1]===c;h+=`<td data-r="${r}" data-c="${c}" class="${hr&&hc?"pick":hr||hc?"hl":""}">${r*c}</td>`}h+="</tr>"}return h+`</table></div><p class="small">Click a number to light up its row and column. Click it again to clear.</p>`}const PV_COLS=["Thousands","Hundreds","Tens","Ones","Tenths","Hundredths","Thousandths"];function pvTable(){const raw=pvVal.replace(/[^0-9.]/g,""),dot=raw.indexOf(".");const whole=(dot<0?raw:raw.slice(0,dot)).slice(-4),frac=(dot<0?"":raw.slice(dot+1)).slice(0,3);let h="<tr>";PV_COLS.forEach(c=>h+=`<th>${c}</th>`);h+="</tr><tr>";for(let i=0;i<4;i++){const k=whole.length-(4-i);h+=`<td>${k>=0?whole[k]:""}</td>`}for(let i=0;i<3;i++)h+=`<td>${i<frac.length?frac[i]:""}</td>`;return h+"</tr>"}function pvHtml(){return`<label class="pv-in">Type a number<input type="text" inputmode="decimal" id="pvInput" value="${pvVal}" autocomplete="off"></label><div class="tool-scroll"><table class="pv" id="pvTable">${pvTable()}</table></div><p class="small">Each place to the left is worth 10 times as much. Each place to the right is worth one tenth as much.</p>`}function strips(){const dens=[1,2,3,4,5,6,8,12],W=600,h=34;let s=`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${W+8} ${dens.length*(h+6)+4}" width="100%" style="max-width:640px" role="img" aria-label="Fraction strips for halves, thirds, fourths, fifths, sixths, eighths, and twelfths">`;dens.forEach((d,r)=>{for(let k=0;k<d;k++){const x=4+k*W/d,y=2+r*(h+6);s+=`<rect x="${x}" y="${y}" width="${W/d}" height="${h}" fill="${r%2?"var(--sea-soft)":"var(--surface)"}" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="${x+W/d/2}" y="${y+h/2}" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" font-size="${d>8?14:17}" fill="var(--ink)">${d===1?"1":"1/"+d}</text>`}});return s+"</svg>"}function numLines(){const rows=[[2,"halves"],[3,"thirds"],[4,"fourths"],[5,"fifths"],[6,"sixths"],[8,"eighths"],[12,"twelfths"]];return`<div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px">`+rows.map(r=>`<div style="text-align:center"><p class="fig-label" style="margin:0">0 to 1 in ${r[1]}</p>${F.numberLine({min:0,max:1,step:1/r[0],hideNumbers:true,ticks:[[0,"0"],[1,"1"]],width:520})}</div>`).join("")+`</div><p class="small">Lining two of these up shows which fraction sits farther from 0, and which unlike fractions land on the same point.</p>`}const frames=list=>`<ul class="starters">${list.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;function sentHtml(st){const p=st&&st.parts&&st.parts[0],k=p&&p.alt&&p.alt.key||p&&p.std&&p.std[0]||"RL.5.1";if(k==="opinion"||k==="W.5.1"||k==="SL.5.4")return frames(["I think ____.","One reason is ____.","For example, ____.","Because of this, ____.","That is why ____."]);if(k==="facts"||k==="W.5.2")return frames(["A deep lake in summer ____.","It happens because ____.","Another thing that matters is ____.","As a result, ____.","So ____."]);if(k==="story"||k==="W.5.3")return frames(["After the fire went out, ____.","At first, ____.","Then ____.","“____,” said ____.","By the end of the half hour, ____."]);if(k==="W.5.8")return frames(["Note: ____ (Source ____)","My sources: 1. ____ by ____. 2. ____ by ____.","The deepest water runs short of oxygen because ____ and ____."]);if(k==="RI.5.9")return frames(["Text 1 says ____.","Text 2 says ____.","Text 3 says ____.","Put together, ____."]);if(k==="SL.5.2")return frames(["This was mostly about ____.","First, ____.","Then, ____.","The part I left out was ____, because ____."]);if(k==="RI.5.1"||k==="RI.6.1")return frames(["The text says, “____.”","I know ____ because the text says, “____.”"]);if(k==="RL.5.4")return frames(["The words “____” compare ____ to ____.","That tells me ____."]);return frames(["The story says, “____.”","I know ____ because the story says, “____.”","The words “____” show that ____."])}const TOOLS={chunk:{name:"Prefix, suffix, and root chart",std:["RF.5.3"],html:()=>F.table(["Word part","What it means"],[["un-, im-, in-, non-","not, or the opposite of"],["re-","again or back"],["pre-","before"],["mis-","wrongly"],["trans-","across"],["sub-","under"],["dis-","apart or not"],["-tion, -ion","the act or result of"],["-able, -ible","can be"],["-ous","full of"],["-ance","the state of"],["flect","bend"],["port","carry"],["struct","build"],["geo","earth"]])+`<p class="small">Cover the prefix and the suffix, read the middle, then put the word back together.</p>`},mult:{name:"Multiplication chart",std:["5.OA.A.1","5.NBT.A.1","5.NF.A.1","5.NF.A.2","5.NF.B.3","5.NF.B.4","5.MD.C.3","5.MD.C.5","6.EE.A.1","6.NS.A.1","6.G.A.2"],html:multHtml,bind:(b,r)=>b.querySelectorAll("[data-r]").forEach(c=>c.onclick=()=>{const row=+c.dataset.r,k=+c.dataset.c;multSel=multSel[0]===row&&multSel[1]===k?[0,0]:[row,k];r()})},pv:{name:"Place value chart to thousandths",std:["5.NBT.A.1","5.NBT.A.3"],html:pvHtml,bind:b=>{const inp=b.querySelector("#pvInput"),tb=b.querySelector("#pvTable");if(inp&&tb)inp.oninput=()=>{pvVal=inp.value.replace(/[^0-9.]/g,"");tb.innerHTML=pvTable()}}},strips:{name:"Fraction strips",std:["5.NF.A.1","5.NF.A.2","5.NF.B.3","5.NF.B.4","6.NS.A.1"],html:strips},nline:{name:"Fraction number lines",std:["5.NF.A.1","5.NF.A.2"],html:numLines},frac:{name:"Fraction method card",std:["5.NF.A.1","5.NF.A.2","5.NF.B.3","5.NF.B.4","6.NS.A.1"],html:()=>`<ul class="starters"><li>Unlike bottoms: list the multiples of each one until a number turns up in both lists, then rewrite both fractions with that bottom number.</li><li>A fraction is a division problem: a/b = a ÷ b.</li><li>Improper to mixed: 23/4 means 23 ÷ 4, which is 5 with 3 left over, so 5 3/4.</li><li>Fraction times fraction: multiply the tops, then multiply the bottoms.</li><li>A whole number can be written over 1: 9 = 9/1.</li><li>“Of” means multiply: 3/5 of 40 is 3/5 × 40.</li><li>Benchmark check: is the answer near 0, near 1/2, or near 1? Does that make sense?</li></ul>`},order:{name:"Order of operations card",std:["5.OA.A.1","6.EE.A.1"],html:()=>`<ol class="starters"><li>Work the innermost grouping symbol first: parentheses ( ), then brackets [ ], then braces { }.</li><li>Inside a grouping, multiply and divide before you add and subtract.</li><li>Rewrite the whole expression after each step so nothing gets lost.</li><li>Check: did the grouping symbols change the order? If not, say so.</li></ol>`},vol:{name:"Volume card",std:["5.MD.C.3","5.MD.C.5","6.G.A.2"],html:()=>F.table(["What to find","How"],[["What volume means","the number of unit cubes that pack the solid with no gaps"],["Volume of a box","length × width × height, answer in cubic units"],["Volume by layers","area of the base × the number of layers"],["A missing height","volume ÷ the area of the base"],["Two solids together","find each volume, then add them"]])+`<p class="small">Volume counts the unit cubes that fit inside. The answer is always in cubic units.</p>`},coord:{name:"Coordinate grid card",std:["5.G.A.1","6.NS.C.6"],html:()=>`<ul class="starters"><li>A coordinate pair is written (x, y).</li><li>The first number moves across, along the x-axis. The second moves up, along the y-axis.</li><li>The origin is (0, 0), the point where the two axes cross.</li><li>Walk before you climb: across first, then up.</li><li>A second number of 0 means the point sits on the x-axis itself.</li></ul>`},sent:{name:"Sentence frames",std:["W.5.1","W.5.2","W.5.3","W.5.8","RI.5.9","RL.5.1","RL.5.4","RI.5.1","SL.5.2","SL.5.4","RL.6.1","RI.6.1"],html:sentHtml},figs:{name:"Simile and metaphor card",std:["RL.5.4"],html:()=>`<ul class="starters"><li>Simile: compares two things using like or as. “The cold came up the way water finds a crack.”</li><li>Metaphor: says one thing is another thing. “The lake at night is a drum skin.”</li><li>Ask: what two things are being compared? How are they alike? What does the comparison make you picture?</li></ul>`},tense:{name:"Verb tense card",std:["L.5.1"],html:()=>F.table(["Tense","How to build it","Example"],[["Past perfect","had + verb","The crew had already left."],["Present perfect","has or have + verb","The log has run out of pages."],["Future perfect","will have + verb","By Friday we will have finished."],["Tense shift","keep one time through the sentence","carried and stacked, not carried and stacks."],["Paired words","not only / but also, both / and, either / or, neither / nor","Not only was it late, but it was also cold."]])}};const SUP_CAVEATS=[{t:"chunk",std:["RF.5.3"],msg:"The chart gives the meaning of the prefixes, suffixes, and roots, so count the word reading as supported, not independent."},{t:"mult",std:["5.OA.A.1","5.NBT.A.1","5.NF.A.1","5.NF.A.2","5.NF.B.3","5.NF.B.4","5.MD.C.3","5.MD.C.5","6.EE.A.1","6.NS.A.1","6.G.A.2"],msg:"The multiplication chart supplies the products. Rate on whether the student sets the problem up correctly and can explain it."},{t:"pv",std:["5.NBT.A.1","5.NBT.A.3"],msg:"The place value chart names the places and lines the digits up, so the student does not have to recall place names. The ten times and one tenth reasoning, and the comparing, still need the student's own thinking."},{t:"strips",std:["5.NF.A.1","5.NF.A.2","5.NF.B.3","5.NF.B.4","6.NS.A.1"],msg:"With fraction strips the student can see the size of each share. Rate on whether the student can write the equation and explain it."},{t:"nline",std:["5.NF.A.1","5.NF.A.2"],msg:"Lined-up number lines show which unlike fractions land on the same point, so count the renaming step as supported, not independent."},{t:"frac",std:["5.NF.A.1","5.NF.A.2","5.NF.B.3","5.NF.B.4","6.NS.A.1"],msg:"The method card gives the common denominator steps, the rule for fractions as division, and the rule for multiplying fractions. Count this stop as supported, not independent."},{t:"order",std:["5.OA.A.1","6.EE.A.1"],msg:"The card gives the order to work the grouping symbols in, so count these answers as supported, not independent."},{t:"vol",std:["5.MD.C.3","5.MD.C.5","6.G.A.2"],msg:"The volume card states what volume means and gives the formula, the layer method, and the missing-height method. Count those items as supported, not independent."},{t:"coord",std:["5.G.A.1","6.NS.C.6"],msg:"The coordinate card names the origin and gives the across-then-up rule, which is most of what this stop asks. Count those answers as supported."},{t:"sent",std:["W.5.1","W.5.2","W.5.3"],msg:"Sentence frames supply the opening, the linking words, and the closing, so count those checks as supported, not independent."},{t:"sent",std:["W.5.8"],msg:"The frames show how to label a note and list a source, so count the list of sources as supported."},{t:"sent",std:["RI.5.9"],msg:"The frames remind the student to draw on each text, so rate on the content of the answer rather than on its structure."},{t:"sent",std:["RL.5.1","RI.5.1","RL.6.1","RI.6.1"],msg:"The frames remind the student to quote. Rate on the accuracy of the quote."},{t:"sent",std:["RL.5.4","SL.5.2","SL.5.4"],msg:"The frames give the shape of the answer. Rate on the content rather than on the wording of the opening."},{t:"figs",std:["RL.5.4"],msg:"The card tells what a simile and a metaphor are, and one of its examples comes from this very story, so count the simile and metaphor questions as supported."},{t:"tense",std:["L.5.1"],msg:"The verb tense card shows how to build each tense and lists the paired words. Count those items as supported."},{t:"passage",std:["RL.5.1","RL.5.4","RI.5.1","RI.5.9","W.5.8","W.5.2","RL.6.1","RI.6.1"],msg:"If the passage was read to the student, this stop shows listening comprehension, not reading."}];return{stops:STOPS,levelups:LEVELUPS,tools:TOOLS,supCaveats:SUP_CAVEATS,passageStd:PASSAGE_STD}}});
